## Mistakes log
Write down only this project's traps that tests · lint · types cannot block. When the user corrected you, a check failed after you said done, a review caught a defect, or you spun on a trap, weigh whether to write it down; a correction no machine can block still gets its line even when the same turn adds a hard rule.
- If a machine can block it, build a test · lint · gate instead of a line. Do not write down habits that are the same in every project or working-style preferences. Keep such lines in auto memory (read only in this repo) and propose them to the user as one line for the global `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.
- One line in that work's commit (on a side branch, separately on main): `- YYYY-MM-DD symptom → cause → what to do next time (file:line) · check: <command>`
- If a written trap is hit again, move it into a test · lint · gate and delete the line. If it truly cannot be blocked, raise it to `Conventions` and delete the line.
- At each release, check that each line's trap still exists. If the file · dependency it points at is gone or a test · gate already blocks it, delete the line (in the same commit as the docs archive).
